## Appendix D Analysis of the RG equations without nesting

The RG equations (9)-(15) involve many variables and parameters, and a full analysis of all nine running coupling constants is rather complicated. Here we analyze the simplest case where there is no Fermi surface nesting other than the BCS channel (\(d_{as}=0\), except for \(d_{1-}=1\) by definition). Then, only \(g_{31}\), \(g_{32}\), \(g_{41}\), and \(g_{42}\) receive one-loop corrections since they can be regarded as BCS interactions. The RG equations for the four coupling constants are written as

\[\frac{dg_{3\pm}}{dy}=-g_{3\pm}^{2}-2g_{3\pm}g_{4\pm}, \tag{10}\] \[\frac{dg_{4\pm}}{dy}=-2g_{3\pm}^{2}-g_{4\pm}^{2}, \tag{11}\]

where we define

\[g_{a\pm}=g_{a2}\pm g_{a1}\quad(a=3,4). \tag{12}\]

We note that \(g_{a+}\) and \(g_{a-}\) characterize spin-singlet and spin-triplet SC, respectively. The RG flow for \(g_{4+}\) and \(g_{3+}\) is shown in Fig. 10. The coupled RG equations can be rewritten as

\[\frac{d(g_{4\pm}+2g_{3\pm})}{dy}=-(g_{4\pm}+2g_{3\pm})^{2}, \tag{13}\] \[\frac{d(g_{4\pm}-g_{3\pm})}{dy}=-(g_{4\pm}-g_{3\pm})^{2}, \tag{14}\]

and thus are readily solved.

When we neglect the exchange interactions, \(g_{4\pm}\) and \(g_{3\pm}\) are reduced to be \(g_{42}\) and \(g_{32}\), respectively. If we further assume that those two intervalley density-density interactions have the same amplitude \(g_{42}=g_{32}\), the equality holds under the RG flow from Eq. (14). Each coupling constant obeys the RG equation \(\dot{g}=-3g^{2}\), which is in accordance with Shankar's result for BCS interactions without nesting [39].

## Appendix E Energy dispersion of a normal state

We construct an analytic form of the energy dispersion with two valley degrees of freedom, belonging to the point group \(D_{3}\) and satisfying a filling condition. For the latter, we require that the energy dispersion have VHS points at the filling \(n=2\). The symmetry conditions are given as follows: The \(C_{3}\) rotational symmetry is kept within a valley, and hence the energy dispersion satisfies the relation

\[\epsilon^{\tau}_{C_{3}\mathbf{k}}=\epsilon^{\tau}_{\mathbf{k}}. \tag{15}\]

There also exist in-plane \(C_{2}\) rotations about the \(\Gamma\)-\(K\) lines, where one of them is taken along the \(k_{y}\) axis. The in-plane \(C_{2}\) rotation interchanges the two valley and flips \(k_{x}\), thus requiring

\[\epsilon^{\tau}_{k_{x},k_{y}}=\epsilon^{\bar{\bar{\sigma}}}_{-k_{x},k_{y}}. \tag{16}\]

A function that obeys the symmetry conditions Eqs. (15) and (16) can be represented as a Fourier series because of the underlying lattice periodicity. Up to the third order, the symmetry-allowed terms are given by

\[\epsilon^{\tau}_{\mathbf{k}}=\sum_{i=0}^{2} \big{[}t_{0}+t_{1}\cos(k^{i}_{y})+t_{2}\cos(\sqrt{3}k^{i}_{x})+ \tau t^{\prime}_{2}\sin(\sqrt{3}k^{i}_{x})\] \[\quad+t_{3}\cos(2k^{i}_{y})\big{]}, \tag{17}\]

where we define \(k^{i}_{y}=C^{i}_{3}k_{y}\) and \(k^{i}_{x}=C^{i}_{3}k_{x}\). The parameters \(t_{1}\), \(t_{2}\), \(t^{\prime}_{2}\), and \(t_{3}\) are real. In addition to the

